  HOW TO READ THIS:
  - If B ~= A (no digit gain from float64 eval alone): the corner-sum
    cancellation in compute_integral() is NOT the bottleneck.
  - If B > A (digit gain, still cheap): switch compute_integral() to
    float64 permanently -- free win, keep training on GPU/float32 as-is.
  - If C > B (float64 training needed to go further): the ceiling is in
    the optimization/representation itself, not just the final sum --
    you'd need float64 training, which is impractical on your GPU; the
    fallback is training on CPU in float64 for final high-precision runs
    only (small nets like [128,128,128] make this tolerable), or look at
    architecture changes (e.g. per-param-set heads) instead.
